Every morning at seven for fifty-one years, Harold Tibbs walked to the white wooden box in his backyard, {{1|opened its louvered door, and he read}} the two thermometers inside. He wrote the readings on a paper form, measured the rain in a narrow brass gauge, and mailed the forms to the National Weather Service at the end of each month. He was one of thousands of volunteer observers whose records form the backbone of the {{2|country's}} climate history.

{{3|Admittedly,}} Tibbs's records were not glamorous. They were, however, {{4|consistent, in}} fifty-one years he missed only nine mornings, each time arranging for a neighbor to take the readings. Scientists {{5|put up with}} this kind of consistency, because a climate record is only as useful as its longest unbroken stretch.

[1] When Tibbs began observing in 1952, his town had fewer than two thousand residents. [2] Today it has nearly twenty thousand, and the fields that once surrounded his yard have become parking lots and subdivisions. [3] Researchers studying how growing towns trap heat have used his records to measure the change. [4] {{6|They found that the town's summer nights warmed faster than the nearby countryside's, a pattern consistent with the spread of pavement.}}

Tibbs stopped observing in 2003, when his eyesight {{7|began failing him and getting worse}}. The box still {{8|stand}} in the yard, its paint peeling and its door no longer opened. {{9|But somewhere in a government archive, more than eighteen thousand of his mornings are kept, each one written down in pencil.}}
